WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.175
This is the last Rules lesson, and it’s the one that turns a pass into a comfortable pass.

2
00:00:06.425 --> 00:00:11.900
The two rule books are nearly the same, so the exam hunts for the places where they aren’t.

3
00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:15.025
Remember your first habit from lesson one?

4
00:00:15.275 --> 00:00:19.675
Find the word Inland, International or Both before you answer.

5
00:00:19.925 --> 00:00:23.350
Today we gather every difference into two tables.

6
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.600
First, sound signals.

7
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:28.075
One and two short blasts.

8
00:00:28.325 --> 00:00:31.775
International: they announce action being taken.

9
00:00:32.025 --> 00:00:35.625
Inland: they propose an intention, and need agreement.

10
00:00:35.875 --> 00:00:37.600
When they’re required.

11
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:42.950
International: whenever a power-driven vessel maneuvers in sight of another.

12
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.875
Inland: when power-driven vessels meet or cross within half a mile.

13
00:00:48.125 --> 00:00:50.025
Overtaking signals.

14
00:00:50.275 --> 00:00:54.875
International: in narrow channels, starting with two prolonged blasts.

15
00:00:55.125 --> 00:00:58.450
Inland: in any waters, just one or two short.

16
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.525
Radio agreement.

17
00:01:00.775 --> 00:01:03.975
International: it doesn’t replace the whistle.

18
00:01:04.225 --> 00:01:06.275
Inland: it does.

19
00:01:06.525 --> 00:01:08.250
Leaving a berth.

20
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.850
International: no signal.

21
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.650
Inland: one prolonged blast.

22
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.825
And the maneuvering light.

23
00:01:16.075 --> 00:01:18.175
International: white.

24
00:01:18.425 --> 00:01:20.700
Inland: white or yellow.

25
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.850
If you remember one line, make it this: offshore I tell you, inshore I ask you.
